// `Debug.inspect` on a Context, and the fault a spent deadline produces. Both
// were live DIFFs between the two backends and neither was visible to anything
// in the tree.
//
// # WHY A FIXTURE AND NOT A CORPUS FILE
//
// context_values_report.nomi states half of it: `tests/` contains no
// program that fails an assertion, so the sweep compares the passing path
// exclusively and reads `0 DIFFED` over any amount of wrong failure text. This
// file adds the other half — a program that FAULTS. A fault is not an assertion
// failure; it ends the run, and the sweep never compares one either.
//
// So this file is not in the corpus, is never run by `nomi test tests`,
// and its non-zero exit is part of what requireIdentical compares.
//
// # THE TWO DIFFS this fixture pins
//
// (1) `Debug.inspect(ctx)` printed `<context>` under `nomi run` and `Context`
// from a `nomi build` artifact. `pub host type Context` gets a name-only
// `impl Debug` from the front end whose body is the static string `"Context"`;
// the interpreter never reaches it, because `debugPlaceholder` answers for the
// non-structural value kinds BEFORE dispatch and returns `"<context>"`. One
// declaration, two implementations, and each backend reached a different one.
// The row path in inspect.go already agreed — which is exactly why a 222/222
// sweep could not see this: `Debug.inspect` in ordinary code is not an
// assertion operand.
//
// (2) A spent deadline reaching main-line code reported
// `deadline exceeded: …` under `nomi run` and
// `concurrent: cancellation reached a frame with no task to unwind` compiled —
// a diagnostic about a BUILDER BUG, handed to a user whose program was merely
// bounded. `rt.EnterDeadline` is a third way a frame's ctx closes, on the main
// goroutine while the body runs, which is the case `raiseCanceled`'s "not
// reachable by construction" argument excluded.
//
// # AN ALREADY-PAST DEADLINE RATHER THAN A SLEEP THAT OUTLASTS ONE
//
// `Instant.from_seconds(0)` is 1970, so the deadline is spent before the
// `timer.sleep` is entered and the fault is immediate on both paths. A future
// deadline plus a long sleep reproduces the same two DIFFs and makes the
// fixture's runtime a race against a wall clock; this way there is nothing to
// wait for. It also exercises `EnterDeadline`'s already-spent path, which is
// the one whose `context.Canceled` used to hide the deadline from the fault.
import {
    std/duration.Duration
    std/instant.Instant
    std/io
    std/timer
}

// The context is read and replaced as an application field, which needs a
// boot to name the application type.
struct Run {
    context: Context
}

fn boot(): Run {
    Run{context: Context.root()}
}

type TraceId String

fn main() {
    // The rendering, at the top level and nested in a container, because the two
    // reach different builder arms — contextInspectCall and debugInspector's leaf
    // — and only the first was found by hand.
    io.print("bare:   ${Debug.inspect(Run.context)}")
    io.print("nested: ${Debug.inspect([Run.context, Run.context])}")

    // A value link is invisible to the deadline walk, so the rendering is
    // unchanged by one and the fault below is unaffected by it.
    traced = Context.with_value(Run.context, TraceId("t-1"))
    io.print("valued: ${Debug.inspect(traced)}")
    io.print("value:  ${Debug.inspect(Context.value(traced, TraceId))}")

    with Run.context = Context.with_deadline(Run.context, Instant.from_seconds(0))
    io.print("spent:  ${Debug.inspect(Context.deadline_remaining(Run.context))}")
    timer.sleep(Duration.seconds(300))
    io.print("UNREACHED: the spent deadline did not stop the sleep")
}
